@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy Policy";
}

@*
    This page is a PLACEHOLDER and deliberately makes no legal claims.

    It previously shipped a third-party boilerplate policy whose content was false for this
    software: it asserted COPPA compliance and that no information is collected from anyone
    under 13, while the node ships parent-linked student accounts and cohorts; it described
    collecting social-security and credit-card numbers, which it does not; and it still
    carried the template's own placeholder contact block. Shipping a pre-written policy an
    operator did not author, and probably would not read, is worse than shipping none.

    The organisation running a node is its data controller. Replace the section below with
    your own policy before serving real users.
*@

<h1>@ViewData["Title"]</h1>

<div class="privacy-policy">
    <hr />

    <section id="privacy-placeholder">
        <p>This node has not published a privacy policy yet.</p>
        <p>
            This software is self-hosted. The organisation running this node decides what
            personal data it collects and how that data is handled, and is responsible for
            describing it here &mdash; the authors of the software are not party to that and
            cannot describe it on an operator's behalf.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted">
            <strong>Operators:</strong> replace this page
            (<code>Views/Home/Privacy.cshtml</code>) with your own policy. What the node
            stores by default &mdash; accounts, cohort membership, xAPI statements, uploaded
            artifacts and their metadata &mdash; is described in <code>README.md</code> and
            <code>docs/ARCHITECTURE.md</code>. That is a starting point, not a substitute for
            a policy written for your deployment and your jurisdiction.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>
